macro_rules! d_EpochFloor_17b18972 {
    () => {
        "\"EpochFloor\":{\"description\":\"The earliest epoch whose storage key a room still lets anybody derive: its chain was pruned below it (`rooms/epoch/prune`), and every epoch under it is meant to be unreadable — at the host, and, as far as each of them conforms, at every member's key holder. Absent means the room's chain has never been pruned.\\n\\n**The off-by-one, stated once.** A rung (`EpochLink`) numbered `e` opens with epoch `e`'s key and yields epoch `e − 1`'s. A prune with `beforeEpoch: N` drops the rungs numbered below `N`, so rung `N` survives and still yields epoch `N − 1`'s key; the floor after it is therefore `N − 1`, never `N`. A party applying a floor `F` discards the rungs numbered `F` and below — rung `F` would yield `F − 1` — and every key for an epoch below `F`.\\n\\n**Monotonic.** A floor only rises. A host raises the room's to the higher of the floor it held and `beforeEpoch − 1` at each prune it performs, and never lowers it; every other party keeps the highest it has learnt for the room, from any source, and never lowers it either. A lower floor learnt later — from a stale response, or a host that has not been pruned — changes nothing.\\n\\n**A host** that holds a floor for a room MUST NOT store, accept or serve a rung numbered at or below it, and MUST serve the floor wherever a response can carry it (`rooms/epoch/chain` 0.2, `rooms/epoch/prune` 0.2).\\n\\n**A member's key holder** — and any client of the member that keeps the room's rungs or keys itself — on learning a floor `F` above the one it held, by any means (`rooms/epoch/chain` 0.2 in its own reads, `rooms/keys/backfill` 0.2, `rooms/keys/chain` 0.2 from its principal):\\n  1. MUST discard every rung it holds for the room numbered `F` or below, and every storage key, file key or other key material it holds or has cached for an epoch below `F` — including one it derived in passing while applying commits through those epochs (`rooms/keys/commit`, `rooms/epoch/commits`);\\n  2. MUST NOT store such a rung or key again from any source — a delivery from its principal, a backfill, a repair, a catch-up, or a host that has not been pruned — and MUST NOT ask for one;\\n  3. MUST refuse to derive or use a key for an epoch below `F`, answering exactly as it answers for an epoch its chain does not reach (`beyondChain`, with an earliest epoch at or above `F`), whatever it still holds;\\n  4. MUST keep `F` itself for as long as it holds anything for the room, since it is what makes items 2 and 3 enforceable after the material is gone.\\n\\nA key holder SHOULD learn the floor whenever it fetches rungs or catches up, and SHOULD ask for it — a `rooms/epoch/chain` 0.2 read carries it whatever `fromEpoch` and `limit` it names — at least as often as it otherwise reads the room. One that never asks never learns, and keeps what it held.\\n\\n**What a floor does not do, stated plainly.** It is honoured by parties that conform; it does not reach into one that does not, and it recalls nothing. Plaintext a key holder already returned, records a member already opened, files already downloaded and decrypted, exports, and any key a member's own software extracted are beyond it. What it guarantees cryptographically is what the prune guarantees: a party that had not derived a key below the floor before the host erased its rungs can never derive it afterwards. What it adds is that every conforming key holder stops being able to — so that a restart, a new device or an agent's next recall reads nothing below the floor, rather than everything its holder happened to keep.\",\"minimum\":1,\"title\":\"EpochFloor\",\"type\":\"integer\"}"
    };
}
pub(crate) use d_EpochFloor_17b18972;

macro_rules! d_EpochLink_3162eb88 {
    () => {
        "\"EpochLink\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"description\":\"One rung of a room's epoch key chain: the storage key of epoch `epoch - 1`, sealed under the storage key of `epoch`. A group key schedule offers no way to derive an earlier epoch's key from a later one — that property is what makes removing a member mean something — so without a chain the first membership change makes every record already in the room unopenable by everyone, including whoever wrote it. The chain is the one-way street run deliberately the other way: a member holding the current key walks it backwards to any retained epoch, and a member holding an earlier key still derives nothing later. Removal stays forward-only; reading stays possible. What a chain costs is stated where it is chosen, in the room's retention policy.\",\"properties\":{\"epoch\":{\"description\":\"The epoch whose storage key opens this link; it wraps the storage key of `epoch - 1`. Never 1: a room's first epoch has no predecessor, so a link claiming one wraps something that is not an earlier epoch's key.\",\"minimum\":2,\"type\":\"integer\"},\"nonce\":{\"description\":\"AEAD nonce, base64url.\",\"type\":\"string\"},\"wrapped\":{\"description\":\"The wrapped predecessor key, base64url. Bound by AEAD associated data to `roomId` and to this link's own position in the chain, so a link lifted to another rung, or served under another room, fails to open rather than yielding a key that is wrong. The binding is load-bearing rather than decorative: every rung is a fixed-length key sealed under a fixed-length key, so nothing about the ciphertext itself says where it belongs.\",\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"epoch\",\"wrapped\",\"nonce\"],\"title\":\"EpochLink\",\"type\":\"object\"}"
    };
}
pub(crate) use d_EpochLink_3162eb88;

macro_rules! d_EvidenceItem_b85c3770 {
    () => {
        "\"EvidenceItem\":{\"additionalProperties\":true,\"description\":\"One evidence item (HCP 4.2.2). An item of a type the provider does not recognize is preserved and never raises assurance (HCP A.3). An item whose type is `verifiable-credential` MUST carry `credential`, an object with `@context`, `type`, `issuer` and `credentialSubject`, held whole as its issuer secured it (HCP 4.2.2). This rule is stated here and not as a schema conditional: the registry's Rust generator does not support `if`/`then`/`else`, so a validator does not check it.\",\"properties\":{\"credential\":{\"description\":\"The credential: for `verifiable-credential`, a W3C Verifiable Credential with `@context`, `type`, `issuer` and `credentialSubject`, whose own data model governs its contents (HCP 4.2.2, 14.2).\"},\"role\":{\"description\":\"`content`: the item's credential is the entry's content (VC Evidence Profile). `supports`: the item backs content stated elsewhere in the entry (HCP 4.2.2).\",\"enum\":[\"content\",\"supports\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"type\":{\"description\":\"The evidence type: `verifiable-credential`, or a URI (HCP 4.2.2, A.3).\",\"maxLength\":2048,\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"},\"verification\":{\"additionalProperties\":true,\"description\":\"The provider's record of its most recent verification of the credential (HCP 4.2.2). Set only by the provider.\",\"properties\":{\"at\":{\"$ref\":\"#/$defs/Instant\",\"description\":\"When the provider verified.\"},\"by\":{\"description\":\"Who verified: the provider, or the Context Subject's trust domain under end-to-end encrypted storage (HCP 4.8). HCP's own values, carried verbatim.\",\"enum\":[\"provider\",\"trust-domain\"],\"type\":\"string\"},\"result\":{\"description\":\"The outcome.\",\"enum\":[\"verified\",\"failed\"],\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"result\",\"at\"],\"type\":\"object\"}},\"required\":[\"role\",\"type\"],\"type\":\"object\"}"
    };
}
